    "id": "574663",
    "title": "IEEEXtreme 20.0 Info Session",
    "description": "About the Session The IEEE Beni-Suef University Student Branch, in collaboration with IEEE Nahda University Student Branch and IEEE Obour CS Student Branch, organized an IEEEXtreme 20.0 Info Session to introduce students to IEEE's global 24-hour online programming competition. The session aimed to provide participants with a clear understanding of IEEEXtreme, its requirements, competition system, preparation roadmap, and the opportunities available through IEEE membership. Topics Covered What is IEEEXtreme? An introduction to IEEEXtreme as a global 24-hour competitive programming competition bringing together university and college students from around the world. IEEE Membership Benefits An overview of the research, networking, career, leadership, competitions, conferences, scholarships, and other opportunities available through IEEE membership. How to Join IEEEXtreme Explanation of IEEE membership requirements, team formation, proctor requirements, and the registration process. Why Participate? The session highlighted the opportunities for improving problem-solving skills, teamwork, competitive programming ability, global ranking, recognition, and earning an official certificate. Required Topics & Skills Participants were introduced to the major areas relevant to the competition, including algorithms, data structures, mathematics, graph theory, number theory, optimization, and logical thinking. Competition Format & Scoring The session explained the 24-hour competition format, multiple test cases, partial scoring, bonus points, leaderboard ranking, and the importance of submission strategy. Rules & Fair Play Important rules were discussed, including responsible use of AI, prohibition of sharing solutions between teams, source citation, and solving independently. Team Building Participants learned how different strengths in algorithms, mathematics, implementation, debugging, communication, time management, and problem selection can contribute to a strong team. How to Prepare The session presented practical preparation steps, including obtaining IEEE membership, forming a team, finding a proctor, practicing on CS Academy, and solving previous IEEEXtreme problems. Key Competition Information Date: 31 October 2026 Place: AASTMT Abuqir Duration: 24 Hours Team Size: 1–3 students Membership: Active IEEE Student or Graduate Student membership is required for every team member. Proctor: Each team requires an IEEE Higher-Grade member as its Proctor of Record. The session concluded by encouraging students to take advantage of IEEE membership opportunities, form their teams, begin practicing, and participate in IEEEXtreme 20.0.",
